00:15:44,432 --> 00:15:46,092
You should now be able to say:

210
00:15:46,432 --> 00:15:50,979
The commit-graph holds generation numbers
for graph walks and changed-path filters

211
00:15:51,019 --> 00:15:52,378
for path-limited history.

212
00:15:52,718 --> 00:15:57,900
It is a cache: stale is slower, not wrong;
grafts, replace objects and shallow

213
00:15:57,940 --> 00:15:59,707
repositories switch it off.

214
00:16:00,047 --> 00:16:03,054
The multi-pack-index is
one index over many packs.

215
00:16:03,394 --> 00:16:08,170
A bitmap tells a server what each selected
commit reaches, and is written by default

216
00:16:08,210 --> 00:16:09,661
only in bare repositories.

217
00:16:10,001 --> 00:16:14,362
The untracked cache and the file-system
monitor replace the working-tree scan of git

218
00:16:14,402 --> 00:16:15,463
status by a question.

219
00:16:17,303 --> 00:16:22,136
Read sections 26.6 to 26.9 of Chapter 26.

220
00:16:22,476 --> 00:16:26,818
Today you gave Git four shortcuts and
every commit ID stayed as it was.

221
00:16:26,858 --> 00:16:32,002
Do the lab before the next video: the fetch
conversation, shallow clones and partial

222
00:16:32,042 --> 00:16:35,705
clones. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

223
00:16:35,745 --> 00:16:37,186
See you in the next one.
